WEBVTT

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.520
I hear you love walk and talks.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.840
Yes, because we sit too much. Yeah.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.200
And also our company is just next

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.640
to this park, so it's great.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.440
Is it all AI talk all the time here?

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.040
I don't know other people's talk,

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.760
but I talk AI all the time.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.120
But that's the only thing I know.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.880
Fei-Fei Li is a giant in
the field of computer vision,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.400
a branch of AI dedicated to
teaching machines to see.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.560
She made a breakthrough with
ImageNet, a visual library

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.040
that sparked the modern AI revolution,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.880
earning her the title, 'Godmother of AI'.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.920
And she's been a pioneer
at AI's center ever since,

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.400
as a Stanford professor, a
Google executive, an advisor

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.000
to US presidents, and
a relentless advocate

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.160
for AI that serves people.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.280
I feel the leaders

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.120
of AI are not talking enough
about empowering humanity.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.720
They're talking too much
about replacing humanity,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.920
replacing jobs.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.440
Now she's adding startup
co-founder to her star resume.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.880
What's it like running your own shop? I

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.360
feel like a tiger mom.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.840
While the rest of the AI
world doubles down on large

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.720
language models like ChatGPT

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.040
and Claude, Li is betting on
a new frontier, world models.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.160
She's building AI that aims to predict

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.080
what happens next in the real world,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.560
not just the next word in a sentence.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.520
So what can world models do ultimately

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.520
that LLMs will never be able to?

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.760
Can words put down fires?
Can words cook an omelet?

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.840
Li and her startup, World Labs,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.080
is competing in a crowded field of rivals,

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.840
all making the same bet

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.120
that world models are
the next big leap in AI.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.600
Did you ever imagine AI would be this big?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.920
Have I imagined the
entire civilization is going

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.880
to be redefined by AI?

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.160
The cultural shifts, the
geopolitical landscape?

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.600
I would not have imagined that.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.000
Before she became a
leading AI scientist,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.200
Li was just a curious
kid looking at the world.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.880
What was young Fei-Fei like as a kid?

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.080
It was '80s and '90s in
China. My city was Chengdu.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.840
My dad is quite a curious
soul about nature,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.760
so I got quite a dosage
of chasing after bugs and,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.440
and running after puppies

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.520
and going to the mountains to, to draw

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.000
and sketch with my dad.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.560
And, you know, reading.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.120
Cutting your hair short,
loving aerospace and physics.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.953
Did you see yourself as a rebel?

00:02:29.953 --> 00:02:30.786
As a kid, right? You're preteen.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.480
You almost have to be a rebel just

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.640
because you want to make
a statement for yourself.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.560
But the love of nature and science

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.880
and physics, the love of aerospace

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.400
and outer space, those are
part of my identity. You

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.000
emigrated from China to the US in - New

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.360
Jersey. In the 90s.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.560
Yeah. What was it like coming here

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.160
as a teenager back then?

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.200
That was tough. Shifting
the whole thing, culture,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.160
language, life

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.240
as a teenager is perhaps
one of the hardest times

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.720
because you're finding who you are

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.600
and then suddenly you don't
even know the world, right?

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.960
Li learned English,
finished high school,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.680
studied physics at Princeton
while running the family dry

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.240
cleaning business on weekends,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.680
and followed her dreams to
Caltech, where she earned her PhD

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.720
and became a computer scientist.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.080
You've been studying artificial
intelligence since then.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.360
It was super niche. Yes.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.080
What
was the field like back then?

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.680
That was a beautiful period.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.600
It was really pure curiosity

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.880
because there was no
money, there was no power,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.960
there was no fanfare, there was no hype.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.320
We were there just the same

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.080
as scientists look into the
sky, go down in the ocean.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.520
I was deeply curious.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.040
How does AI see

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.600
and how is it different
from how you or I see?

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.240
We use our eyes to
drive the sensory system

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.880
to turn that into signals in the brain,

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.480
and the brain does computing.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.400
It's not that different for computers.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.720
We need to learn the pattern of the world

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.720
and understand objects,
colors, environments.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.440
A lot of seeing is
preparing humans to act.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.840
You wake up this morning,
you hug your kids,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.080
you go downstairs, prepare
some breakfast, grab your key,

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:18.400
drive to your work.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.880
Everything I've described
so far, you've done that

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.760
with your spatial intelligence. So

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.080
it's got to be hard to
get computers to do that.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.480
Yes.
Li was working on this back in 2006.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.880
She built ImageNet, a massive catalog

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.440
of 14 million pictures in
over 21,000 categories,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.760
the largest assembled at that time,

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.800
because Li saw that algorithms
needed data to get smarter.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.040
So what was the spark for ImageNet?

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.280
Nobody was paying attention to data.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.840
My students and I had that
epiphany that learning needs

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.440
to be driven by data.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.840
In 2010, Li turned
ImageNet into a competition.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.560
Who could build the best algorithm

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.000
to correctly identify the most images?

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.880
In 2012, Geoffrey Hinton's
University of Toronto team entered

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.080
with AlexNet, an algorithm
powered by NVIDIA Graphics cards.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.840
The result marked a turning point.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.360
That combination, massive
amount of data, neural networks,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.520
and GPU computing power
became the golden recipe

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.360
for modern AI and cemented Li's legacy.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.160
What do you feel when you look at this?

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.960
It's not the eight of us.
It's the entire humanity.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.040
That's really the main story here.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.600
Because this comes from
the photo of, what,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.600
about a hundred years ago when
industrialization was in the

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.200
booming phase and big urban
skylines are being built.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.520
That was a civilizational moment.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.680
It's amazing that you're
being recognized here,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.920
but like, could they
have centered the photo?

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:54.680
Come on. It's true because
there's more space here.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.360
I asked you about being the godmother

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.193
of AI a few years ago. You

00:05:58.193 --> 00:05:59.026
did. How

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.400
do you feel about that title?

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.440
You know, Emily,

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.400
I would never call myself
godmother of anything.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.440
Remember, Emily, when you
asked me, I was like, oh.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.840
I was taken aback

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.240
because I don't naturally
think about myself

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.720
as godmother of anything.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.480
That's not my personality.
I focus on work.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.440
If I rejected that on
that spot, we once again

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:24.880
would be in a situation

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:29.280
where women just don't get
recognized the same way as men.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.760
I want more women to be
called godmother of whatever

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.560
creation, innovation,
discovery that they have done.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.440
So do you embrace the term now or?

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.360
Embrace is a big word.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.760
I don't go around and
say, "Hi, I'm Fei-Fei

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.760
I'm the godmother of AI."
But people have adopted

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.800
that term thanks to you.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.440
Artificial intelligence had
long been discussed in policy

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.480
and tech circles as something perpetually

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.160
right around the corner.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.400
Until ChatGPT, AI went
from a niche research field

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.120
to the most talked about
technology on the planet.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.920
Large language models, image
generators, video generators.

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.080
Suddenly it seemed like everyone was

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.320
or wanted to be in the AI business.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.960
And let's be honest, things
have come pretty far.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.280
Li saw an opportunity and
launched her own startup in 2024.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.720
What's it like running your own shop?

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.320
So here's the thing. I'm

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.640
by far the most senior in the company.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.880
From my co-founders to my
engineers and researchers

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.320
and scientists, they're so
talented, but they're also young.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.080
I do feel like a mom.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.720
And oftentimes I feel like a tiger mom.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.360
I do run this shop as, I
would say, high standard.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.840
Li is working on
advancing world models.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.360
If ImageNet taught AI how
to see, this technology aims

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.280
to guide AI through the physical world

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:53.960
because describing how to catch a ball

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.280
and actually catching one
are very different things.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.240
And super intelligence,
she's betting that lies

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.760
beyond chatbots.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.480
The rest of the AI industry
is focusing on LLMs.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.080
What can't LLMs do?

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.000
If we want to create a
world where we continue

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.120
to push scientific discovery,

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.680
where we want machines like
robots to be a partner to us,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.360
all this language cannot do it alone.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.600
Building world model

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.240
and spatial intelligence
is not about anti - LLM.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.360
It's about the next frontier
and the next chapter.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:33.360
So what exactly is a world model? World

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.920
model at this point
is an overloaded term.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.240
We look at spatial intelligence

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.160
as three different kind of functions.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.040
One is rendering, one is simulation,

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.080
and the third one is planning.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.360
Rendering is the world model outputs

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.280
beautiful pixels for humans to consume.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.160
Most likely you're thinking
about [OpenAI's] Sora.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.840
The second class of world
model is they do simulation,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.480
but it doesn't serve just humans.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:04.880
It serves machines.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.560
They are focused on capturing
the actual structure

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.120
of the world, the geometry
structure based on physics.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.160
The third kind of world model
planning helps you to know

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.080
what is the next thing you need to do.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.800
For example, if you have
a cup, a world model

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.640
that serves as a planner will
let the robot know, pick it up

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:28.800
and put it somewhere else.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:34.320
And that is a world model
that is very closely coupled

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:35.840
with robotics.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.000
Marble is World Lab's first step towards

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.360
creating a world model.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.400
It's a platform that lets
anyone generate an explorable,

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.320
editable 3D world from a
single visual or text prompt.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.840
Now, this is a showcase page
of some user-generated world.

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:54.760
You can see they created
a room, a beautiful room.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.880
Now you look at this,
you're like, Fei-Fei,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.960
this is no different from a picture.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.280
But here's the thing.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.160
It actually is a world
because I can navigate in it.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.760
So like a game, I can move
forward. I can turn around.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.680
I can go anywhere.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.920
Here, it's a fully consistent 3D world.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:17.160
So who or what companies are using

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.360
Marbel right now?

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.400
Why is Marble useful? For
example, virtual production.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.120
In movies, people are using Marble

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.160
because they can shoot
actors in any environment.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.600
Game developers.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.560
Marble has completely lowered
the amount of resource

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.360
and time one needs.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.400
We also have robotics example.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.360
This is a collaboration with NVIDIA where

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.560
they're using Marble environments

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.480
to augment the training of robots.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:47.880
What are you training these models on

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.400
that others don't have?

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.920
We are training these
models on two things, right?

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.440
One is specially prepared data.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:00.040
Pixel data are more nuanced than

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:01.880
language data in the sense that

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.000
there's more information in a picture.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.920
There's camera information.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.800
Another one is algorithmic
and architectural innovation

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:13.920
because no one has created

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.440
generative world models as we
have in terms of creating 3D

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.440
and eventually 4D worlds. So would

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.000
you say that's the secret?

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.200
Yeah, I guess so.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.640
Well, the real secrets are
people, as I always say.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.640
But yes, the data and the algorithm

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.880
and the, the system we have built.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.040
You've raised a billion dollars.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:35.760
How is the business doing? The

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.000
business is still early.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.920
This phase of World Labs,

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.280
we're focusing on building the technology.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:43.960
Building the technology
isn't the only part of the job.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.160
When Li isn't heads down at World Labs,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:49.040
she's out making the case
for the next frontier of AI.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:58.200
Absolutely.
Please welcome to the stage.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.200
Fei-Fei Li, co-founder

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:02.880
and CEO at World Labs for a conversation

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.880
with Bloomberg's Emily Chang. All

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.080
of this rolls up into
robotics, so I, I want

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:08.240
to get your take on the field.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.760
And humanoids in particular.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:12.600
Funding for humanoids hit $6 billion,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.680
but you know, they still can't load my

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.160
dishwasher as fast as I can.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:18.120
They still can't go
get my Amazon packages.

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.760
Will world models,
world labs close the gap

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.240
between hype and reality? That's

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:25.600
a loaded question, Emily.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.200
First of all, first of all,
robotics is going to be one

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.120
of the most important revolution
in human industrialization.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.520
$6 billion is too small, right?

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.120
If you look at self-driving
cars investment,

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.160
if you look at language models investment,

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.240
it took way more than $6 billion.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:45.800
Are we going to close the gap?

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.880
I do believe World Labs is working on one

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:51.920
of the most critical technology in the

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.120
spatial physical intelligence.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.640
And obviously that's
the, that's the hope. But

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:58.640
Li isn't alone.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.680
World models are the tech
industry's latest obsession

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.000
with startups and tech giants alike all

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.440
sprinting to build them.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:09.000
For anything from design to
self-driving cars, robots

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.560
and factories, to eventually machines

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.840
that can navigate the
unpredictable reality

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:14.840
of the human home.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.080
You're competing against big
players with deep pockets.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.160
How worried are you about that? As

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.880
an entrepreneur and a co-founder, CEO,

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.240
I'm paranoid every day.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:32.400
I don't think there's a moment
I would say, "Hey, I feel

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.880
so relaxed." You know?

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.880
So yes, I am worried, but am
I paralyzed? Absolutely not.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:43.960
We have an incredible team.
We also have the focus.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:47.320
You know, a lot of big
companies, they have

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.400
a lot on the, on the table.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.640
We have one thing on the table,

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.920
and we're focusing on that thing. Emily,

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:56.800
Nice to meet you.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.520
The war for talent is so fierce.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:01.120
Why work with Fei-Fei
when there are so many

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.320
powerful AI companies out there? Well,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.520
I started working with Fei-Fei
way before it was cool in

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.440
2012. Yeah, I did my PhD

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.120
with Fei-Fei at Stanford
way back in the day.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.440
You can go to a lot of big tech,

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.880
but your hands can't change

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.640
that trajectory in, in many instances.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.480
Here,
we're 50 people.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.960
Your hands can change the
trajectory of this company.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.360
That's a beautiful thing
to have in your life.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:27.040
World Labs may be a small team,

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.040
but the investment in
world models is gigantic.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:33.840
$3 billion and growing.
Still, it's early days.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.800
The field hasn't even agreed
on how to build them yet.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.840
Would you say it's like 2019 for chatbots

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:41.760
where everyone was chasing LLMs

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:42.840
and trying to make it happen,

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:44.480
but they hadn't cracked the code yet?

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.480
Is that where we are for world models?

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.360
Yeah, that's probably a
fair description. We're early.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.320
We're a lot earlier compared to LLMs. But

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.000
Will there be some sort of explosion

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.920
and aha moment where we're like,
"Oh, now we understand." If

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.120
we do it right, yes.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.600
That's the magic of
technology and science.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.440
When it hits that milestone,
it becomes magical. There

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.640
are skeptics in the AI

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:10.120
and robotics world that say,

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:11.880
"This isn't the next breakthrough.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.080
It's just the next hype cycle."
What do you say to that?

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.680
I think even a good
science can be hyped.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.760
I believe spatial intelligence

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.640
and physical intelligence are fundamental

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:26.520
to machine intelligence.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.080
And these are the frontier questions

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.920
that I believe firmly in as a
scientist, as a technologist,

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.080
and also as an entrepreneur.

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.600
I believe they will provide incredible

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:39.880
market value. Building

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:42.080
wrld models is incredibly
resource intensive.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.800
Do you need more power? Do you
need more capital to get to

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:46.760
where you want to be? That

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:47.760
we don't know yet.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:49.960
We're too early. We're
likely to need more power

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:51.640
and more resources.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:53.360
And can you get that?
I don't know.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.920
We have to do a good job to, to earn it.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:15:59.040
Getting world models out

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.360
of the demo phase will
take more time and money.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:04.600
Meanwhile, investment in
generative AI has soared,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.040
and Americans have
strong feelings about it.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.360
I'm sure you've seen the
video of former Google CEO,

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.120
Eric Schmidt, getting booed
at a college graduation.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:14.520
So today we stand on this edge

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.680
of another technological transformation.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.760
You spend a lot of time with
students. What are they saying?

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:27.560
And if they're scared, are
the fears justified? Yeah,

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:29.680
I do spend a lot of time with students.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.520
Even Stanford students reflect
some of this mixed sentiment.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.240
There is anxiety. There is sense of hope.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:41.040
There is also excitement.
There is also confusion.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:44.760
There is also simultaneously
a sense of dignity

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.640
and agency when AI can help me do things

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.080
that I couldn't do before.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.120
And a sense of loss of dignity

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.040
and agency if AI is going to take my job.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:57.447
AI
Has

00:16:57.447 --> 00:16:58.393
Got to go.

00:16:58.393 --> 00:17:01.600
So what happens if the US
loses faith in this technology

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:04.520
while the rest of the world races ahead?

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:09.240
US is such an important
country in terms of its impact

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:14.240
to the world that if we are not showing a

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:15.600
positive attitude

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.800
and positive path towards
AI, everybody loses.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.200
And I don't wish for that

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:24.960
because US has been a thought
leader in the world when it

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:28.280
comes to technology and
science and economy.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:33.280
And if we can show that we can
create incredible technology

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:37.120
and incredible economic value using AI

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.880
and have a positive cultural impact,

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:43.200
the world will see it as a role model. Li

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.680
knows a thing or two
about AI and its impact.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.560
Over her career, she took
roles at big tech companies,

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:49.840
venture capital firms,

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:52.240
co-founded an AI institute at Stanford,

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:53.800
and advised US presidents

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.400
and the United Nations on AI policy.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:59.960
There's nothing artificial
about artificial intelligence.

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:03.600
It's inspired by people.
It's created by people.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:07.400
And most importantly, it
has an impact on people.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:11.600
You've advised President
Biden, President Trump, the UN

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:13.720
on technology and AI policy.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:17.440
How do you approach conversations
with world leaders? I

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:18.560
don't go in hyping.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.200
I don't go in providing extreme rhetoric.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:26.800
Either it's total utopia
or total doomsday.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:30.880
It's very important that
whoever I talk to knows

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:32.560
that I'm a scientist.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:35.640
What do they need? They
need scientific facts,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:40.080
but I think it's important
to be at their service

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:44.040
and to speak my mind, to
push back if they're wrong,

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:46.320
because that's my responsibility.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.880
And have they been wrong?
Oh, yes, for sure.

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.680
We all have been wrong. This
technology moves so fast.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:55.000
I don't blame them. Most

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.280
of our policymakers don't come from

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:59.240
computer science background. What

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:00.280
is the role of regulation?

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:02.120
What should that look like?

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:04.160
First and foremost, root

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.720
that in science, not science fiction.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:11.040
A lot of the AI policy world conversation

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:15.880
with people from Silicon Valley
talk about the extinction

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:20.560
of humanity, the, the,
the AGI machine overlord

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:23.200
that distracts the real policy work.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:28.120
The second recommendation is
ensure a healthy ecosystem,

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:29.520
resource the public sector,

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:35.360
resource STEM education in K-12 or K-16.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.840
Human capital at the end

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:41.320
of the day is the most
important resource of our world.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:44.280
And we want a thriving society of people,

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.040
not a thriving society
of just machines. What

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.520
do you worry could go
wrong with world models?

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:52.040
Like if we're giving
people even more realistic

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:54.480
and more powerful AI, does

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:57.040
that create even more opportunities

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:58.920
for really bad things to happen?

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:02.240
World models, language models,
these are powerful models

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:05.400
that can create mis and disinformation.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.600
Empowered robots can be
weaponized by bad actors

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:10.800
and students who

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:14.360
are not properly motivated
can use technology

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:19.080
as a lazy crutch instead of
a empowering learning tool.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.160
I, I worry about that a lot.

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.240
So I think a lot of things can go wrong

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:24.920
with technology, not just AI.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:28.600
It could have gone wrong with
electricity, with cars, with,

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:31.200
you know, PC, with internet. Some

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:33.920
of the most powerful CEOs
in AI right now have been

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:36.560
accused of having God complexes

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:38.600
because they seem to think they know

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:40.200
what's best for humanity.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:42.080
What do you think of that term? I

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:44.600
think it's dangerous for any individual

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:47.120
to think they know better
than everybody else.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:51.680
As a CEO, as an entrepreneur,
our founding premise is

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:54.680
we are here to contribute to the society

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:56.880
and to empower people.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.480
It's not our position

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:01.640
to make every single
decision for people. But

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:02.880
isn't that the path we're on?

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:03.880
I mean, these are really

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:06.200
powerful people.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:09.320
I think it's too early to tell
Emily. I'm not being naive.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:13.160
I know what you're talking
about. I also have my concern.

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:16.600
I don't think having God
complex is constructive,

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:18.680
and I wouldn't want to do that myself,

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.080
but we have a democratic society.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:24.760
This is another reason I
think our civil society should

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:25.960
not give up on AI.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:30.440
We need the voices. We need
the collective governance.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:33.680
But let's now throw the
baby with the bathwater.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:36.560
The governance shouldn't
be, let's stop AI,

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:41.280
because this is a technology
that can discover cure for

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.840
diseases, can empower students

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.800
and teachers, can help our elderly.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.240
So that's the baby here.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:52.120
But right now, we are
in a very messy period.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:57.120
This is also why I feel
personal responsibility

00:21:57.520 --> 00:21:59.760
to speak the truth I believe in

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.120
as a scientist and an educator.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.960
Li has spent her life
teaching machines to see.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.040
She gave AI its eyes.

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:09.120
Now in her second act, she's trying

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:11.040
to give it a world to live in.

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:14.200
As the scientist who helped
spark the modern AI revolution,

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:16.560
perhaps we shouldn't put it past her.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:20.040
Where is this all going? Where
are we in five years? This

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.680
This is where I reveal I'm an optimist.

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:28.440
The, the arc of history
is long. I paraphrase Dr.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:31.200
King. I believe it bends
towards benevolence.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:36.560
Now, we're not a perfect world of people

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:41.400
and community, but 2026
today is better than

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:46.400
2026 BC for the majority
of the population.

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:50.960
And I think it's because despite
all of our dark underbelly,

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:52.880
we have it in our DNA.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:56.200
We want our offsprings
to live a better life,

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:00.440
and we have moral compasses collectively

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:03.720
to move the society towards benevolence.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.240
My hope is in humanity.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.200
Is it true you're
obsessed with UFOs? When I

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:23.480
was a kid. Like,

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:26.680
Have you read the documents
they're declassifying?

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:27.680
No.

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:31.960
I think it's part of
that dreamy curiosity

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:34.000
as a kid is what's out there. Do

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.240
you think there's aliens out there? So

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.920
as a physics student,
you learn Drake's equation.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:43.280
Drake's equation computes
the probability of potential

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.840
of outer space aliens.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:48.680
So there is a potential number. Wow.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.920
Okay. Favorite sci-fi movie?

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:54.120
Contact. By far my
favorite. There you go.

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:56.600
Intelligence...
somewhere out there. Yeah.

